### Why imperfect institutions can persist

It would be a mistake to conclude that, because an institution *could* reduce a transaction cost, it will automatically appear wherever that problem exists. Roland calls the inference from a thing's present **function** to the cause of its **origin** the **functionalist fallacy**. His microwave example makes the logic clear: an invention's later use need not have been its designer's original aim. Similarly, political settlers seeking protection from tyranny may create rules that later support commerce, even if promoting growth was not their stated purpose. Knowing how a rule works and knowing why it arose are separate questions. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Roland - chapter 7 (Institutions) - single page.pdf#page=24|Roland, PDF p. 24 (printed p. 198)]], [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Roland - chapter 7 (Institutions) - single page.pdf#page=25|Roland, PDF p. 25 (printed p. 199)]]

Inefficient institutions can last because replacing them requires people to organize, bear costs and overcome those who benefit from the existing arrangement. Weak courts, insecure property rights and restrictions on association can themselves make reform harder. Coordination creates an additional hurdle: each person may wait for others to adopt a better pattern before changing their own behaviour. Thus observing a bad rule does not prove no better rule is possible, and observing a useful rule does not prove it arose because it was efficient. The chapter particularly emphasizes collective-action barriers and imperfect democracy; it does not supply a universal account of every institution's history. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Roland - chapter 7 (Institutions) - single page.pdf#page=22|Roland, PDF p. 22 (printed p. 196)]], [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Roland - chapter 7 (Institutions) - single page.pdf#page=24|Roland, PDF p. 24 (printed p. 198)]], [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Roland - chapter 7 (Institutions) - single page.pdf#page=25|Roland, PDF p. 25 (printed p. 199)]]

The chapter locates this argument in an institutionalist tradition. Veblen stressed status-motivated consumption and social norms; Commons treated transactions and legal rules as central; Coase examined transaction costs, the firm and property rights; Williamson developed asset specificity and hold-up; North emphasized long-run property-rights institutions; and Olson explained free riding in collective action. These are conceptual connections supplied by Roland, not six independent empirical tests in this chapter. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Roland - chapter 7 (Institutions) - single page.pdf#page=25|Roland, PDF p. 25 (printed p. 199)]], [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Roland - chapter 7 (Institutions) - single page.pdf#page=26|Roland, PDF p. 26 (printed p. 200)]]


Origin: reused complete checked passage [[01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Comparing_Economic_Systems.md#Systems change, and institutions feed back on themselves]]; fingerprint `8764485b6a043a0bcf7003eea29bf86eaa00b3cb42d43f2fde4d15cb696a2778`. Heading depth and required relative figure-path relocation alone are adapted.

### Systems change, and institutions feed back on themselves

The lecture rejects a single inevitable route from tradition to feudalism to capitalism or from central planning to one market model. It uses China and Russia to illustrate different, politically shaped transition paths toward forms it calls state capitalism. **Path dependence** means that earlier arrangements alter the resources, expectations, and power of later decision makers; a reform starts from that inherited position. A related **survivorship bias** arises if we infer the original form of all traditional societies only from those that still survive. A system change may lack a deliberate starting decision or a clear start date; where there was a decision, the new system could have been imposed rather than voluntarily chosen, as with Soviet-bloc central planning. The design and survival of a system can also serve political goals, so political control can help stabilize it even when its economic results are poor for much of the population. These are interpretive claims from the slides, not proof that one historical path is universal. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Week I - lecture - shorter - large slides.pdf#page=31|Week I lecture, PDF slides 31–36]]

![Narrow Corridor schematic in the Week I lecture](../../../../../01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/assets/Week_1_lecture_p34_narrow_corridor.png)

*The lecture's Narrow Corridor schematic* places state power vertically and society's power horizontally. It labels a corridor where state capacity is constrained by organized society as the **Shackled Leviathan**; outside it are a **Despotic Leviathan** where state power dominates, an **Absent Leviathan** with weak state capacity, and a weak-both **Paper Leviathan**. The country labels are illustrative placements in a qualitative model, not measured coordinates or a necessary sequence of development. This visual connects political constraints to economic institutions; the course's uploaded *Why Nations Fail* chapter supplies a different historical argument, so one should not pretend that chapter itself contains this figure. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Week I - lecture - shorter - large slides.pdf#page=34|Week I lecture, PDF slides 34–35]]

![Institutional feedback schematic in the Week I lecture](../../../../../01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/assets/Week_1_lecture_p44_institutional_feedback.png)

*The lecture's Acemoglu–Robinson (2006) feedback diagram* links today's political institutions to **de jure** (legally assigned) power, and today's distribution of resources to **de facto** (effective) power. Together those forms of power help shape today's economic institutions and tomorrow's political institutions. Economic institutions affect economic performance and the next resource distribution, which feeds back into later power. The arrows express a conceptual causal mechanism and endogeneity, not measured coefficients or algebraic identities. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Week I - lecture - shorter - large slides.pdf#page=43|Week I lecture, PDF slides 43–44]]

#### Why a striking comparison may still mislead

![Austria and Czechoslovakia historical GDP per person chart](../../../../../01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/assets/Week_1_lecture_p40_austria_czechoslovakia_gdp.png)

*Austria–Czechoslovakia comparison.* The chart plots GDP per person in **1990 international Geary–Khamis dollars** over 1820–2010 using the Maddison Project 2013 version. The visible gap later in the series motivates a question about divergent institutions and systems. It does not isolate a causal effect of socialism: war, borders, industrial structure, measurement, and other historical differences also require analysis. The slide retains a “Czecho-slovakia” series for 2000 and 2010, after the state's dissolution, without explaining how those observations were constructed. Read its units, source date, and this labeling limit before using it as evidence. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Week I - lecture - shorter - large slides.pdf#page=40|Week I lecture, PDF slide 40]]

![West and East German historical growth comparison table](../../../../../01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/assets/Week_1_lecture_p41_gdr_growth_comparison.png)

*Germany measurement example.* For 1950–1989, the slide reports West German GNP and GNP-per-person growth figures of 4.2 and 3.6, while its East German official **NMP** series reports 5.6 and 5.9. Two re-estimates of East German GNP using different 1989 Mark/DM conversions report 1.6/1.9 and −2.2/−1.9 respectively. The table's labels do not print a unit for these rates, and NMP and GNP are different aggregates. The key lesson is sensitivity to accounting and conversion, not a precise uncontested ranking from unlike numbers. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Week I - lecture - shorter - large slides.pdf#page=41|Week I lecture, PDF slide 41, table attributed there to Pohl (1991)]]

The lecture surveys geography, culture, and leaders' ignorance as proposed explanations of prosperity, then poses Nogales and other counterexamples to simple one-factor accounts. It argues that trust and cooperation can themselves be shaped by institutions. These examples show why competing explanations must be examined; they do not mean geography or culture never matters. The [[01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Colonial_Institutions_and_Divergence.md|Nogales and colonial-history note]] explains the uploaded chapter's own evidence and its limits in detail. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Week I - lecture - shorter - large slides.pdf#page=37|Week I lecture, PDF slides 37–43]]

Finally, the lecture's Lagos insecurity scenario, Adam Smith quotation, and China discussion all point to the same decision mechanism: if property claims, contract enforcement, or predictable treatment are doubtful, an investor has reason to protect or conceal resources, seek personal connections, or use shorter-horizon opportunities. The China slide specifically names inconsistent rules, weak independent judicial checks, and reliance on *guanxi* networks; those are lecturer claims for discussion, not a blanket characterization of every Chinese transaction. The uploaded Roland chapter explains the contracting problems behind that intuition. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Week I - lecture - shorter - large slides.pdf#page=46|Week I lecture, PDF slides 46–52]] [[01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Institutions_and_Economic_Development.md|Institutions and economic development]]

![Article screenshot on relatives affected by criminal convictions](../../../../../01_Notes/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/assets/Week_1_lecture_p52_collective_punishment_article.png)

*The lecture's final example* is a screenshot of a September 2024 article describing a debated practice in which a relative's conviction can restrict access to certain public jobs, university programs, or military service. It illustrates an **indirect effect** of legal rules on people who were not convicted and why households may care about predictability and equal application. The screenshot reports nearly 25 million recorded offenders during 2000–2022 and states that **some 100 million people could be at risk** if each has three affected relatives. That multiplication gives about 75 million relative instances before any overlap; the article does not explain the difference. Neither number is an observed count of people actually penalized. The slide presents this as an article excerpt, not as independently verified course data. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Comparative_Economics/Week_1/Week I - lecture - shorter - large slides.pdf#page=52|Week I lecture, PDF slide 52]]


## Worked example

### O006-W1

**Item/version:** `O006-W1` / 1. **Role:** worked example. **Components:** C1 persistence mechanisms, C2 function versus origin, C3 power-feedback and corridor, C4 path dependence and inference limits.

**Prompt:** Hypothetical: an old licensing rule restricts entry; favored firms fund officials. Most citizens favor open entry but expect others to bear lobbying costs and fear being first. Explain persistence, origin/function distinction and both lecture diagrams; evaluate “all societies must progress along the same path.”

**Prepared key and essential reasoning:** Existing privilege creates concentrated beneficiaries and diffuse participation costs; expectations can keep reformers at a low-action pattern. A license may currently screen quality, but that function alone does not prove why it was created. Legal office rules define de jure power, wealthy firms supply de facto influence, together affecting entry rules and future politics; profits/resource distribution can reinforce influence. Corridor axes state/society power and four qualitative labels do not assign this country a measured location. Path dependence constrains reform without fixing it; transitions vary, may be imposed/no single start, and universal sequence is unsupported. The vignette is hypothetical mechanism, not an identified case.

**Item decision:** Pass requires every listed component in the key with no critical error. Partial means some valid reasoning but a missing essential component or a local slip; fail means the defining mechanism is wrong. Accept paraphrase and different examples only where explicitly allowed by the key. 

## Practice with fading assistance

### O006-S1

**Item/version:** `O006-S1` / 1. **Role:** supported completion. **Components:** C1 persistence mechanisms, C2 function versus origin, C3 power-feedback and corridor, C4 path dependence and inference limits.

**Prompt:** Completion: a weak court encourages a small trusted network; people want broader trade but wait for others to switch. An incumbent profits from the network. Fill collective/coordination/beneficiary mechanisms, explain why present trust function is not origin evidence, reconstruct feedback and corridor axes/labels, and state two historical-inference limits.

**Prepared key and essential reasoning:** Collective reform costs/free riding, waiting for reciprocal adoption, and incumbent profit can sustain the arrangement. Trust’s present useful function cannot explain origin by itself. Formal political rules ->de jure power; resources ->de facto power; both ->economic rules/current and next political rules; economic outcomes/next resources feed back. Corridor: state vertical/society horizontal, Shackled constrained-capacity corridor, Despotic state dominance, Absent weak state, Paper weak-both. Labels qualitative, no measured causal coefficients or inevitable route. Earlier arrangements shape possibilities but do not forbid change; surviving examples cannot reconstruct all original systems.

**Item decision:** Pass requires every listed component in the key with no critical error. Partial means some valid reasoning but a missing essential component or a local slip; fail means the defining mechanism is wrong. Accept paraphrase and different examples only where explicitly allowed by the key. Notes allowed. Hint 1: Separate costs of organizing from costs of a lone switch. Hint 2: Keep legal authority and effective influence distinct; follow the diagram across time. Then show the worked step if still stuck; this response cannot establish independence.

### O006-I1

**Item/version:** `O006-I1` / 1. **Role:** full_scope_independent (immediate). **Components:** C1 persistence mechanisms, C2 function versus origin, C3 power-feedback and corridor, C4 path dependence and inference limits.

**Prompt:** Hypothetical licensing vignette: connected incumbents profit; citizens favor reform but wait for others to organize and adopt new rules. Explain three persistence barriers, function/origin distinction, full de jure/de facto feedback chain and all four Narrow Corridor labels/axes. Distinguish path dependence from permanence and note survivorship-bias or imposed-transition limit.

**Prepared key and essential reasoning:** Incumbent resistance, costly collective organization/free riding and conditional coordination can block a better rule. A current screening function does not prove original motive. Political institutions ->de jure power; resource distribution ->de facto power; both ->current economic institutions and next political institutions; economic institutions ->performance/next resources ->later power. State power vertical/society horizontal; Shackled constrained capacity, Despotic state dominance, Absent weak state, Paper weak-both. Qualitative placements/arrows not estimates, chapter attribution distinct. Path dependence shapes options/power not permanence; surviving traditional cases bias origin inference, or change can be imposed/no single starting decision.

**Item decision:** Pass requires every listed component in the key with no critical error. Partial means some valid reasoning but a missing essential component or a local slip; fail means the defining mechanism is wrong. Accept paraphrase and different examples only where explicitly allowed by the key. 

### O006-I2

**Item/version:** `O006-I2` / 1. **Role:** full_scope_independent (immediate). **Components:** C1 persistence mechanisms, C2 function versus origin, C3 power-feedback and corridor, C4 path dependence and inference limits.

**Prompt:** A hypothetical ineffective property registry benefits brokers; reformers face organizing costs and distrust adoption by others. Explain persistence, reject “the registry exists to raise efficiency, so that proves its origin,” trace the lecture feedback and corridor axes/four labels, and qualify an inevitable transition claim using path dependence and survivorship or imposed-change reasoning.

**Prepared key and essential reasoning:** Brokers defend benefits, reform has collective costs/free riding and adoption coordination/trust barriers. Current function cannot establish historical origin or automatic efficient emergence. De jure from political rules and de facto from resources jointly shape economic/current and next political rules; outcomes/next resource distribution reinforce later power. Corridor axes state vertical/society horizontal; Shackled, Despotic, Absent and Paper meanings as above, qualitative and not this chapter’s diagram. Inherited position alters later incentives without deterministic sequence; survival selection hides lost forms, or imposed/gradual transitions complicate deliberate start dates.

**Item decision:** Pass requires every listed component in the key with no critical error. Partial means some valid reasoning but a missing essential component or a local slip; fail means the defining mechanism is wrong. Accept paraphrase and different examples only where explicitly allowed by the key. 

## Rubric and independence

The prepared keys operationalize each component; all are essential. Pass requires an explicit mechanism and justified application plus every specified limit. Partial applies to an incomplete argument or isolated arithmetic transcription error with otherwise correct setup; teach gives feedback and obtains a fresh check. An arithmetic slip is not itself proof of conceptual failure, but a numerically wrong result does not count as a complete pass. Fail applies to any critical conceptual error identified below. Unscorable applies to missing response or ambiguous/unknown help conditions; do not infer acquisition.

**Full role coverage:** I1, I2, D1, and D2 each test all components C1 persistence mechanisms, C2 function versus origin, C3 power-feedback and corridor, C4 path dependence and inference limits. Normal acquisition is two distinct passes on I1 and I2, same revision/criterion and known unassisted conditions. Permitted repair/entry substitution: use a previously unseen D1 or D2 as a `full_scope_independent` check; record that immediate use explicitly and do not count it as delayed retention. Only combinations of two different unexposed I/D items are permitted. Supported, worked, diagnostic, and C items do not fill this role. Correction items are deliberately narrower and cannot substitute for acquisition or delayed checks. If both delayed variants are consumed during repair, request new prepared delayed items from plan before claiming delayed readiness.

Each delayed pass independently requires the same entire criterion after at least 24 elapsed hours from the last instruction/solution exposure for this objective, with current tuple, fresh item and known intervening exposure. The 24-hour guard is an operational minimum; recall owns dates. A previously seen question can be used for practice only.

## Misconceptions and correction

**Observable sign:** Learner says current usefulness proves efficient intentional creation. A single response supports a tentative diagnosis; it becomes established only with corroborating reasoning, never from hesitation alone. **Corrective explanation:** A consequence or present function and a historical cause are separate propositions; require origin evidence. **Hint 1:** Name the distinction being tested. **Hint 2:** Compare the alternatives using the supplied definitions; after two ineffective hints show the relevant worked step.

### O006-C1

**Item/version:** `O006-C1` / 1. **Role:** fresh correction; practice only. **Components:** C2 function versus origin.

**Prompt:** Fresh correction: a rule now improves commerce but was reportedly adopted to restrain tyranny. Does commerce explain its origin?

**Prepared key and essential reasoning:** No; present commercial function can differ from original political purpose, and actual origin needs historical evidence.

**Item decision:** Pass requires every listed component in the key with no critical error. Partial means some valid reasoning but a missing essential component or a local slip; fail means the defining mechanism is wrong. Accept paraphrase and different examples only where explicitly allowed by the key. 

**Observable sign:** Learner calls a corridor label a measured coordinate or path dependence an inevitable sequence. A single response supports a tentative diagnosis; it becomes established only with corroborating reasoning, never from hesitation alone. **Corrective explanation:** The diagrams map qualitative mechanisms; inherited constraints still permit multiple paths and reforms. **Hint 1:** Name the distinction being tested. **Hint 2:** Compare the alternatives using the supplied definitions; after two ineffective hints show the relevant worked step.

